90% (score 390). Structure understood and mostly matching; only register-allocation/scheduling minutiae left.

Semantics (right):
  typedef struct { s32 a,b,c; } V12;              // 12-byte struct, see my project note
  typedef struct { u8 pad[0x94]; s16 f94,f96,f98,f9A; } Obj;
  s32 func_00295490(s32 arg0, s32 arg1, Obj *arg2, V12 *arg3) {
      V12 local; s32 r;
      local = *arg3;
      arg2->f94 = -100; arg2->f96 = -100; arg2->f98 = -100; arg2->f9A = 0;
      r = func_001441B8(0x30, arg1, local.c);          // 2nd arg is arg1 (caller's $a1, still live)
      if (r != 0) func_00294308(r, &local, arg1, arg2, arg0);
      return r;
  }

What differs from the ROM:
1) Register allocation of the 12-byte copy: ROM uses $v1 (ldl/ldr/sdl/sdr) and $a2 (lw 8 -> sw 8),
   i.e. the copy's third word IS the 3rd argument of func_001441B8 - ONE 'lw $a2,0x8($a3)' feeds both
   'sw $a2,0x8($sp)' and the call. Best attempt allocates the copy to $v0/$v1 and re-loads the argument
   from the stack ('lw $a2,0x8($sp)'), or (with arg3->c) emits two separate loads. Writing local = *arg3
   with the argument written as local.c scores best; arg3->c scores worse (470).
2) Because the ROM does not clobber $v0 in the copy, the literal -100 stays live in $v0 from the very
   top of the function (2nd instruction, before the sd saves). In my code the copy takes $v0 so the
   constant is materialised after the copy. Fixing (1) probably fixes (2) automatically.
3) Store order: ROM = 0x98, 0x94, 0x96, then 'sh $zero,0x9a' in the jal delay slot. Mine always puts the
   0x9a zero-store first. Tried several source orders of the four stores - GCC normalises them, no luck.
Tried: field-by-field copy of the struct (much worse, 1565, no ld pair), stores before/after the copy,
a local variable holding -100, packed struct. Keep local = *arg3 + local.c as the argument.

ee-gcc 2.96 -O2 and the address of a local pointer (cost me func_0013AAC0): if you write
  char *tmp;  ... f(a, &tmp); g(b, tmp); f2(a2, &tmp); g2(b2, tmp);
the compiler treats &tmp as reg_equiv_address and rematerializes 'addiu $a2, $sp, off' before
each call, so the pointer never gets a callee-saved register (3 saved regs, no $s2).
Put the buffers and the out-pointer in ONE local struct and pass &l.s:
  struct { char b0[0xA0]; char b1[0xA0]; char *s; } l;
  func_00138758(arg0, arg1[1], &l.s); func_00138900(l.b0, l.s);
  func_00138758(arg0, arg1[2], &l.s); func_00138900(l.b1, l.s);
That compiles to addiu $s2, $sp, 0x140 + move $a2, $s2 before each call: the extra $s2 save.
The idiom is everywhere in 0x138000-0x13C000 (func_00138E00, func_00139230, ...), all of which
keep &tmp in $s2/$s3 across func_00138900. Also: these look-up functions do take two parameters
(a0 is the table passed straight to func_00138758) even though m2c only shows one.

Score 10/99.6%, 28/28 insns. Working source:
s8 D_00483FFC; s8 D_00483FFD; s8 D_00484010;
void func_00166D50(void){ func_00191BA8(0xFFFFFF); while(D_00483FFC!=0){ func_00165238(); func_00191180(); } func_00191BB0(); if(D_00483FFD!=0){ func_002E4368(); D_00483FFD=0; D_00484010=1; } }
ONLY diff: ROM loop-back = lb $v1 / bnez $v1; ours = lb $v0 / bnez $v0. Constant is 0xFFFFFF not -1. See note on function for all variants tried.

Best score 10 (2 regalloc: constant 0x28 scratch is $v0 in our build, $v1 in ROM; everything else identical, 19/19 insns). Working source (score 10):
void *func_001449A8(s32); void func_003021B0(void*,s32,s32);
typedef struct { u8 pad_00[0x9A8]; s64 unk9A8; } TmpBig;
void *func_002EB790(void){ TmpBig *p=func_001449A8(0xA08); if(p==NULL) return p; func_003021B0(p,0,0xA08); p->unk9A8=0x28; return p; }
NOTES: (1) The early-return form ("if (ptr==NULL) return ptr; ... return ptr;") is required: the plain "if (ptr!=NULL){...} return ptr;" form produces a wrong branch-target layout (0x38 vs 0x3c, score 20). (2) Layout/count/operands are exact; only the li scratch reg differs. Tried without effect ($v0 every time): early-return, explicit return in both branches, void* ptr + cast, TmpBig* return type, long/u64 field, sizeof(TmpBig) struct of 0xA08, extra local copies, ternary/comma. Target materializes 0x28 into $v1 (addiu $v1,$zero,0x28; sd $v1,0x9a8($s0)). Suspect the original has an extra live value in $v0 (or a different allocator state). Info from caller func_002EB0F8: return value is a 32-bit pointer stored into fields 0x14/0x18 of the caller's object (sw), alloc size 0xA08, field zeroed by func_003021B0 (=memset).

Arrow/entity descriptor init: a->unk0 = D_00478188 (name str 'firearrow'); a->unk4 = 0; a->unk8 = 8; a->unkC = 0x1E (u16, sh); a->unk60 = D_00451400 (144 bytes data ptr). Store order in ROM: 0xC, 0x8, 0x60, 0x0, delay: 0x4. Best attempt (score 30, ONE displaced instruction, struct: u8*unk0, s32 unk4, s32 unk8, u16 unkC @0xC, pad, u8* unk60 @0x60): source exactly
  a->unkC = 0x1E; a->unk8 = 8; a->unk60 = D_00451400; a->unk0 = D_00478188; a->unk4 = 0;
Mine emits lui a1,lui a2, [lo a1, lo a2, addiu v0=0x1E, addiu v1=8]; ROM wants lui a1,lui a2, addiu v0=0x1E, lo a1, lo a2, addiu v1=8. I.e. gcc hoists the two lui's (found: try declaring D_00451400 as s32[] and fields as u8* gives this) and everything else matches. Only the addiu $v0,$zero,0x1e lands one slot late. Tried: statement orders C/8/60/0/4, C/60/0/8/4, pointers-first, pointer-typed vs s32+cast fields, 8/C reversed order. Scheduling tie-break never flipped. Suspect the original computes the 0x1E constant some way that makes gcc schedule it before the two %lo adjustments (or the source has an extra no-op statement), or a slightly different field type at unk4/unk8.

Best 90/91.8% (2 instructions' worth of diff). Structure: struct { char* name; s32 a; s32 b; s16 c; char _pad[0x52]; void* data; } at $a0; sets c=0x1E (0xC), data=&D_0044D7F8 (0x60), name=D_00463218 (0x0), b=0 (0x8), a=0 (0x4).

The best source I found (score 90) is:
  extern u8 D_0044D7F8[];
  extern char D_00463218[];
  p->c = 0x1E; p->data = &D_0044D7F8; p->name = D_00463218; p->a = 0; p->b = 0;
Remaining diff (exactly 2 problems, both ordering): (1) target emits `sh $v0,0xc($a0)` AFTER the address setup, we emit `sw $v1,0x60($a0)` there instead; (2) the target's `sw $v1,0x60($a0)` comes after the `sh`, dead zone.
What is left: GCC insists on scheduling the two pointer stores before the halfword store (it treats 0x60 and 0x0 as the live values and pushes the 0x1E store down). I tried 20+ statement orders (all permutations of the 5 stores), u16/s16/volatile, an s16 local, a pointer-cast version (X3), an inline helper, and a macro. Nothing moved the sh earlier. Suspect the original wrote it as a compound literal / memset, or as an initializer through a different base, or the fields are assigned via a local struct built on the stack (which would explain the dead slot after the const but no stack usage appears in the target, so probably not).

SIF status wait sequence. Source shape is RIGHT and verified (best 1380, 61.6%):
  void func_002D5570(void) {
      s32 t;
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;
      <BARRIER>;                 /* sync */
      if (t != 0) func_002DD698();
      while ((*(vu32 *)0x1000F000 & 4) == 0) { }
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;
      <BARRIER>;                 /* then tail-calls func_002DD698 if t */
  }
  The '*(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;' interleaves exactly right by itself (lui $v1,0x1000 / addiu $a0,$zero,4 /
  ori $v1,$v1,0xf000 / sw $a0,($v1)), and the wait loop plus the 16-byte loop alignment padding come out
  right too, so once a barrier exists this should drop to 0.
  BLOCKER: the 'sync' instruction (raw ROM word 0x0000000F, verified at 0x002D5590). __asm__ is rejected;
  __sync_synchronize() expands to a libcall 'jal __sync_synchronize' (and that symbol doesn't link).
  Needs an inline-asm barrier or a project macro - see also the note on func_002D5D60.

Same SIF wait pattern as func_002D5570 - BLOCKER is the 'sync' instruction, see that function's notes.
Shape (from m2c + the sibling) should be:
  s64 func_002D5600(void) {
      s32 flag = 0; s64 out;
      func_002D5450(&flag, &out);
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;  <BARRIER/sync>
      if (t != 0) func_002DD698();
      while ((*(vu32 *)0x1000F000 & 4) == 0 && flag == 0) { }
      t = func_002DD640();
      *(vu32 *)0x1000F000 = 4;  <BARRIER/sync>
      if (t != 0) func_002DD698();
      return out;   /* out at sp+8, flag at sp+0, frame 0x20 */
  }
  Note the last store to 0x1000F000 is materialised as 'lui $1,0x1001; sw $3,-0x1000($1)' - i.e. gcc folds
  the 0xF000 low half into a negative offset with hi+1, which falls out naturally from the same constant.
  Note also: the local 'flag' must be initialised to 0 BEFORE the func_002D5450 call (it is an out-param).

Best attempt kept: score 190 (94.2%), 34 insns vs 33 target. STRUCT PASSED BY VALUE to func_001441B8 IS CONFIRMED: a 12-byte struct (3 s32 or 3 f32) copied from *arg3 to a stack local with ldl/ldr/lw + sdl/sdr/sw. The struct type determines nothing (s32, u32 and f32 variants all compile identically), so just use 'typedef struct { s32 a,b,c; } Thing;'.
Body: s32 func_0029AF78(s32 arg0, s32 arg1, s32 arg2, Thing *arg3) {
          Thing local; s32 temp; local = *arg3;
          temp = func_001441B8(0x1C, local);
          if (temp != 0) { func_0029B0D0(temp, arg1, arg2, arg0); }
          return temp; }
WHAT IS LEFT - two small things, both regalloc:
(a) The ROM's prologue saves arg0 in $s0, arg1 in $s1, arg2 in $s2 and passes them to func_0029B0D0 as $a0=temp,$a1=arg1,$a2=arg2,$a3=arg0. GCC instead assigns $s0=arg2, $s1=arg1, $s2=arg0 and emits an extra 'move $a1, $sp' (it passes the struct local by address because func_001441B8's prototype lacks the by-value struct). That extra 'move $a1, $sp' is the 1 extra insn, and the shuffled s0/s2 assignments are the regalloc diffs. If the prototype is written as 'extern s32 func_001441B8(s32, Thing);' gcc DOES pass it by value (ldl/ldr/lw/sdl/sdr/sw) - it is only when the caller cannot see a by-value struct prototype that it passes a pointer. So: declare func_001441B8 in the real header with a by-value struct (or as taking the same struct the caller has) and the extra 'move $a1,$sp' disappears; the s0/s2 assignment order is then the only thing left.

