void return + fp-assigned-after-the-last-call is what gives `jal` instead of a sibcall

BEST 160 (84%). The whole function is: s32 r = func_003062C8(); then a normal (non-tail) call func_002FBC20(r) with a jal + ld $ra/jr $ra epilogue.
THE SIB-CALL PROBLEM: every direct spelling (void func(){func_002FBC20(func_003062C8());} , s32 return version, temp-variable version, unprototyped callee) makes gcc emit a tail call 'j func_002FBC20' with no frame restore -> 8 insns, score 460. Blocking it works with a LOCAL FUNCTION POINTER:
  extern s32 func_002FBC20(s32); extern s32 func_003062C8(void);
  void func_003062F0(void) { s32 (*fp)(s32); s32 a = func_003062C8(); fp = func_002FBC20; fp(a); }
-> 9 insns, score 160. (Declaring the fp in the initializer, or using void return types, or passing the nested call directly through fp, all give jalr + a $s0 save instead - the temp variable + separate assignment is what folds it back to a direct jal.)
REMAINING 2 INSNS: gcc puts the argument move INSIDE the second jal's delay slot (jal func_002FBC20 / move $a0,$v0) while the ROM has move $a0,$v0 / jal func_002FBC20 / nop. So it is only the delay-slot filler choosing the CLD parallel move. Anything that stops gcc from treating 'move $a0,$v0' as a sinkable regmove (so it stays glued to the call) should give score 0 - I did not find one. Worth trying next: passing the value through something the scheduler cannot sink (e.g. an extra use of 'a' in the same block), or a call with a second constant arg to see if the CLD moves stop being sunk.

