97.2% (score 30, 1 instruction out of place). BIG WIN for the next agent: make all five stores VOLATILE and GCC stops reordering them. Best source now scores 30 (was 90 for 4 agents / 31 attempts):

  extern u8 D_0044D7F8[];
  extern char D_00463218[];
  typedef struct { char *name; s32 a; s32 b; s16 c; char _pad[0x52]; void *data; } Thing5E970;
  void func_0015E970(Thing5E970 *p) {
      *(volatile s16 *)&p->c = 0x1E;
      *(void *volatile *)&p->data = &D_0044D7F8;
      *(char *volatile *)&p->name = D_00463218;
      *(volatile s32 *)&p->b = 0;
      *(volatile s32 *)&p->a = 0;
  }

That reproduces every instruction except the position of `addiu $v0,$zero,0x1e`: the target has it 3rd (between the two %hi lui's and the two %lo addiu's), we always emit it 5th, immediately before its `sh`. Note the source order of the trailing zero stores must be b (0x8) then a (0x4) - the compiler then keeps that order and puts 0x4 in the delay slot.

Tried and did NOT work: 20+ statement orders, struct-field vs pointer-cast forms, s16/u16/volatile mixes (c volatile + others plain = worse, 250), computing the two addresses into locals first, pointer-cast form without volatile, chain assignment, register variables. The constant is always materialised adjacent to its sh store (GCC expands the move_immediate at reload time), so it never lands at position 3 that way. Untested ideas: writing the 0x1E through a hard `register` variable declared first; some construct that makes GCC treat the two %hi lui's as one unit with the constant. Suspect the original used a volatile store for 0xc plus a slightly different RTL shape.

UNMATCHABLE in pure C (like the syscall stubs): the target needs one COP1 RSQRT instruction (.byte 0x96,0xb5,0x00,0x46 = rsqrt.s fd,fs,$f0, i.e. 1/sqrt(sum of squares)) that no C construct emits. Verified in one compile: neither `__builtin_rsqrtf`, `__builtin_rsqrt` nor `rsqrtf` exist - GCC 2.96 treats them as external calls (all three reported as 'not a known symbol'). `1.0f/sqrtf(x)` becomes a call to sqrtf (also unknown) or sqrt.s+div.s.

The C is otherwise fully worked out: it builds a quaternion from a 16.16 fixed-point angle and a direction vector. func_0015A070 = sin_fx16 (called first, result stored to q->w at +0xC), func_0015A100 = cos_fx16. Structure that matches everything else:
  void func_00159E00(Quat *q, s32 a, f32 fa, f32 fb, f32 fc) {   /* f12,f13,f14 -> fb/fc */
      s32 ang = (a << 16) >> 17;          /* sll $s0,$a1,16 ; sra $s0,$s0,17 */
      f32 sum = fa*fa + fb*fb + fc*fc;   /* target order: mul y*y into $f2, x*x into $f0, z*z into $f1, then add.s f0,f0,f2 and add.s f0,f0,f1 */
      q->w = func_0015A070(ang);
      f32 s = func_0015A100(ang) * (1.0f/sqrtf(sum));
      q->z = s * fc;  q->x = s * fa;  q->y = s * fb;   /* stores in order +8, +0, +4 */
  }
Target detail: the 1.0f for the reciprocal is materialised early (lui $at,0x3f80 ; mtc1 $at,$f22 at slots 10-11, before the integer work) and used only by the rsqrt, so the constant disappears when the rsqrt goes.

STRUCTURE FULLY RECOVERED, score 10 stable (only 2 regalloc diffs), 21/21 insns.
Target: p=func_001449A8(0xF0); if(p==NULL) func_002FA010(p,0,0xF0); func_003021B0(); *(void**)((char*)p+4)=(char*)p+0xE0; return p;
Working source (score 10):
  void *func_001449A8(s32); s32 func_002FA010(void*,s32,s32); void func_003021B0(void);
  void *func_002FA060(void){ void *p=func_001449A8(0xF0); if(p==NULL){func_002FA010(p,0,0xF0);} func_003021B0(); *(void**)((char*)p+4)=(char*)p+0xE0; return p; }
THE ONLY DIFF: ROM does "daddu $a1,$0,$0; daddu $a0,$s0,$0" (a0 = p) for the func_002FA010 call, but the harness cprop folds the argument to $zero on the p==NULL path (ours: "daddu $a0,$0,$0; daddu $a1,$0,$0"). Everything else (frame, saves, branch, delay slot, func_003021B0, store) matches byte for byte.
Tried with no effect (all still score 10): void*/u8*/char* p; s32 p + (s32) cast; s32/s32/s32 prototype; void(void*,s32,s32) prototype; void() no-arg prototype; cast (void*)p; (u32)p==0; !p; p==0; extra local copies (q=p); variable zero arg. NOTE: func_002EB790 (0x2EB790) in the same family also stalls at score 10 on a scratch-register pick, so this 0x2Exxxx/0x2Fxxxx helper family seems to have compiler-state quirks.
NEXT IDEA for whoever picks this up: find a source shape whose operand in the call is NOT provably 0 on that edge (e.g. a value clobbered by func_001449A8 so cprop can't fold). If the ROM compiler simply did not fold, this one may be unmatchable with the harness gcc.

func_0013C230 (0x13C230) BEST 125 (43 vs 44). Only remaining diff: the loaded object pointer is
placed in $a3 instead of $a2, adding one extra `move $a2,$a3` (which also shifts the beql target).

WHAT MAKES THE SCHEDULE MATCH (all of it matches at 125):
  if (*(void **)((u8 *)arrp) != NULL) {
      for (;;) {
          u8 *off;
          obj = *(void **)((u8 *)arrp + (i << 2));   // obj = arr[i]
          t = *(u8 **)(obj + 0x48) + 8;
          off = obj + *(s16 *)t;
          fp = *(void (**)(void *, s32, void *))(t + 4);
          fp(off, 3, obj);
          i++;                                        // <-- AFTER the call
          if (*(void **)((u8 *)arrp + (i << 2)) == NULL) break;
      }
  }
- i++ MUST be after the call. Putting it right after the load gives score 160; inline `(i++ << 2)`
  gives 145. This is the only form that stops gcc strength-reducing the arr[i] walk and keeps the
  target's exact instruction order (addiu a1,3; lw obj; addiu s1,1; ...).
- Use the char* form `(u8 *)arrp + (i << 2)` (not `arrp[i]`): a pointer base makes gcc emit the
  base-first `addu $v0,$s0,$a0` the ROM has; `arrp[i]` emits offset-first `addu $v0,$a0,$s0`.
- Frame/stack need the explicit `Locals` struct { u8 buf[0xA0]; s32 spA0; s32 pad[3]; void *arr[28]; }
  (-> frame -0x140, arr at sp+0xB0, s0@0x120 s1@0x128 ra@0x130). Plain separate locals give -0x150.

THE BLOCKER (regalloc): obj is loaded into $a3 then copied to $a2 for the call. ~55 source variants ALL
reproduce $a3: obj typed u8*/void*/u32, every local-declaration order, struct-field (`->vt`) access,
third arg passed as obj / re-read from the array / a separate copy / (void*)obj, fp prototyped
(void*,s32,void*), (u8*,s32,void*), (void*,s32,u8*) and old-style `void (*fp)()`, with/without an off
temp, i typed s32/unsigned, `arrp[i]` vs `*(T*)((u8*)arrp+(i<<2))` vs `+(i*4)`, and prologue
permutations. Suspect an ee-gcc 2.96 global-alloc/regmove artifact: the arg setup move `(set $a2 obj)`
is not coalesced into obj's definition, so obj stays in $a3. `register ... asm("$6")` is rejected by
the tool (inline assembly). Someone should try a genuinely different construction (e.g. the object
reaching the call as a memory operand) or accept it as unmatchable.

