  typedef struct ent { s32 key; s32 pad1; struct ent *sub; s32 pad2[11]; struct ent *next; } ent;
  typedef struct node { s32 pad0[2]; ent *first; s32 pad1[2]; struct node *next; } node;
  typedef struct tree { s32 pad[10]; node *root; } tree;
Target shape (19 insns): n=root; if(!n) return NULL; e=n->first; GUARD beql e,0 -> outer test (delay: n=n->next); val=e->key; INNER bnel val,key,-> bottom test (delay: e=e->next); jr; move v0,v1 (== return e); bottom test: bnel e,0,-> the val-load block (delay: val=e->key); n=n->next; outer bnel n,0,-> the guard (delay: e=n->first); jr; move v0,zero (return NULL). So: the inner loop is BOTTOM-tested (rotated: the e->key reload sits in the back-edge delay slot) and the key comparison branches OVER the inline `return e` epilogue.
What gets you there: the outer shape is easy (nested for/while over node/entry, beql/bnel guards fall out automatically). Two mutually exclusive results, 20+ compiles:
 (a) `while (e) { if (e->key == key) break; e = e->next; } if (e) return e;`  -> 930 (51%): compare sense RIGHT (bnel val,key,continue with `jr; move v0,v1` inline) but GCC does not rotate the inner loop: back edge goes to the guard and there are 3 extra nops; also 3 branch-target operands differ because of that.
